Fusing prior knowledge with data-driven learning is attractive precisely
where data is scarce, yet no controlled account says when such fusion helps,
when it is redundant, and when it harms. We present a
benchmark that measures one fixed, hand-crafted source of knowledge, a
pinned bank of Gabor targets injected only during training at $\sim$2\%
compute overhead, against the data-driven alternatives (SimCLR, SimSiam and
DINO pre-training, ImageNet transfer, heavy augmentation, learned teachers)
under one frozen recipe with committed subsets: 12 datasets, 9 backbones, data scales from
500 to 1.28M images, resolutions from 32 to 224\,px, and models from 5.7M to
86M parameters ($2{,}800$ experimental cells). Every pairwise combination
is measured too, and follow one rule: different-\emph{currency}
sources stack (prior with augmentation), same-currency sources substitute
(prior against effective self-supervised pre-training), and fusing into an
already-informed initialization interferes in proportion to what it carries
(ImageNet transfer, up to $-17$ points). Because each source's currency is
measurable on frozen features before the combination is built, the outcome
is predictable in advance, which we state as a procedure. The rule holds
where the sources are two Sentinel-2 sensor sets and where they are two
trained classifiers, not only where one is a prior. Underlying it is an empirical decomposition,
$\Delta = G + \mathrm{readout}(\mathrm{base})$, whose sign holds on $79\%$
of testable cells and which, registered in advance, called an unseen
backbone family's feature gain from baseline heights alone. All artifacts are released at
\href{https://github.com/GCVCG/MomentAux}{\texttt{github.com/\allowbreak GCVCG/MomentAux}}.
